{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The configuration-index harness completed on a clean copy (CheckRun dd34591ecee5442ca8f7b8c478709f1b, exit 0) and the decisive parsed event 0 witnesses the defect on one real node: on the elected leader of a bootstrapped three-node cluster, the main thread held latest/committed configuration index 1 with 3 voter servers, while the same instance's public GetConfiguration().Index() returned 0 (Configuration() carried the same 3 voters) and Stats() reported latest_configuration_index=\\\"0\\\" beside a non-empty latest_configuration, with commit_index 3 and applied_index 3. The declared discriminator resolved in the predicted direction, so the account moved from inferred to witnessed: GetConfiguration() responds a configurationsFuture built only from the atomic latestConfiguration copy (api.go:900), getLatestConfiguration transfers no index (raft.go:2232-2240), and Index() returns that missing field (future.go:267-270), while the real index exists in-process and is released only over configurationsCh (raft.go:889). The consequence stays bounded and is stated as such: the returned configuration is live and no consensus decision rests on this value, but a caller that takes the index from the only public source and passes it back as prevIndex supplies 0, which nextConfiguration treats as no precondition, silently disabling the documented configuration compare-and-swap. I recorded one execution-backed fact, answered the open gap with the receipt and parsed index, updated the investigation with its explicit reliance on the three saved behaviors, listed the honest counterweight (the tree is byte-identical to the released upstream copy, no in-tree assertion requires a non-zero index) and the single-sample limit. The second investigation on duties lost when authority is lost by Shutdown() rather than step-down remains open with its discriminator and no execution this turn.\"}"}}
